      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x+6\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(4,16)?$",
      "worked_solution": "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x+6\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot x-3+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2x-3+3{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =2(4)-3+3(4)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =8-3+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\frac{13}{2}.$",
